public static String canTravelMaze(int n, int[] start, int[] end, int[][] paths) {
    boolean[] visited = new boolean[n];
    Queue<int[]> queue = new LinkedList<>();
    queue.offer(start);
    visited[start[0]] = true;
    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
        int[] current = queue.poll();
        for (int[] next : paths[current[0]]) {
            if (!visited[next[0]]) {
                visited[next[0]] = true;
                queue.offer(next);
                if (current[0] == end[0] && current[1] == end[1]) {
                    return "can be travelled";
                }
            }
        }
    }
    return "cannot be travelled";
}
```

Explanation:

This function takes in an n-teresting space of size n, the starting and ending n-tersection coordinates, and the paths between adjacent n-tersections. It uses a breadth-first search algorithm to check if it is possible to travel from the starting n-tersection to the ending n-tersection. It returns "can be travelled" if the path exists, and "cannot be travelled" otherwise.

The function initializes a visited array to keep track of the n-tersections that have been visited, a queue to hold the n-tersections that need to be explored, and a bool to check if the ending n-tersection has been reached.

It then loops through each n-tersection in the queue and adds each of its adjacent n-tersections to the queue if they have not been visited. If the current n-tersection is the ending n-tersection, it returns "can be travelled".

If the queue becomes empty and no path has been found, the function returns "cannot be travelled".

This function assumes that the n-teresting space is connected, meaning that there are paths between all n-tersections. If this is not the case, the function may not be able to find a path from the starting n-tersection to the ending n-tersection.

