public int findMinFibonacciNumbers(int k) {
    int[] fib = {1, 1};
    int i = 0;
    while (fib[i] < k) {
        int next = fib[i] + fib[i + 1];
        fib = Arrays.copyOf(fib, fib.length + 1);
        fib[i + 1] = next;
        i++;
    }
    int min = 0;
    while (fib[i] >= k) {
        min++;
        k -= fib[i];
        i--;
    }
    return min + 1;
}
```

This is a classic dynamic programming problem. The key is to find a recurrence relation that can be used to calculate the minimum number of Fibonacci numbers needed to sum up to k. The recurrence relation is as follows:

Let dp[i] be the minimum number of Fibonacci numbers needed to sum up to i. Then dp[i] can be expressed as follows:

dp[i] = min(dp[i - F1], dp[i - F2], dp[i - F3], ..., dp[i - Fn]) + 1,

where F1, F2, F3, ..., Fn are the first n Fibonacci numbers.

The base case is when i = 1, we only need 1 Fibonacci number to sum up to i, which is F1.

The solution to the problem can be found by calculating dp[k] using the recurrence relation. Here is the complete method:
```java
public int findMinFibonacciNumbers(int k) {
    int dp[] = new int[k + 1];
    int F[] = new int[k + 1];
    F[0] = 1;
    F[1] = 1;
    for (int i = 0; i <= k; i++) {
        dp[i] = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
    }
    for (int i = 2; i <= k; i++) {
        for (int j = 1; j < i; j++) {
            dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], dp[i - j] + 1);
        }
        F[i] = F[i - 1] + F[i - 2];
    }
    return dp[k];
}
```
