public int maxUniqueSplit(String s) {
    int n = s.length();
    int[][] dp = new int[n][n];
    int max = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j <= i; j++) {
            if (i - j < 2) {
                dp[i][j] = 1;
            } else {
                dp[i][j] = 2;
                for (int k = j + 1; k <= i - 1; k++) {
                    if (s.charAt(j) != s.charAt(k) && s.charAt(i) != s.charAt(k)) {
                        dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i][j], dp[k - 1][j] + dp[i - k][k]);
                    }
                }
            }
            max = Math.max(max, dp[i][j]);
        }
    }
    return max;
}
```
This solution uses a dynamic programming approach, where `dp[i][j]` represents the maximum number of unique substrings that can be formed by splitting the string from `s[j]` to `s[i]`. The recurrence relation is as follows:
```
dp[i][j] = 1, if i - j < 2
dp[i][j] = 2, if i - j >= 2
      = max(dp[i][j], dp[k - 1][j] + dp[i - k][k]), if i - j >= 2 and k is the index of a split point between substrs s[j] and s[i] that is not between s[j] and s[i - 1]
```
The base case is when `i - j < 2`, in which case we can only split the string into two substrings, so `dp[i][j] = 1`.

The solution iterates over the length of the string `n` and the starting index `j` of the substring `s[j:i]`, and updates `dp[i][j]` based on the recurrence relation. The maximum value of `dp[i][j]` across all value of `i` and `j` is stored in `max` and returned as the final answer.

The time complexity of the solution is O(n^2), and the space complexity is O(n^2) for the `dp` array.

